            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Willy Brandt</strong><br>\nWilly Brandt was born in 1913 in Lübeck, Germany. His original name was Herbert Ernst Karl Frahm, and he became known as Willy Brandt only after emigrating to Norway to escape Nazi persecution for his socialist beliefs and activities. As a young man, he was deeply involved in socialist politics and opposed the Nazi regime.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Brandt's Political Beginnings</strong><br>\nIn the 1930s, Brandt, still known by his birth name, actively participated in the Socialist Youth movement and later in the Social Democratic Party. His political work was directed against the growing influence of the Nazis, which forced him to flee Germany in 1933 due to the risks involved in his activism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Exile and War Years</strong><br>\nAfter leaving Germany, Brandt took on the alias Willy Brandt to avoid Nazi persecution. He spent the war years in exile, working as a journalist in various Scandinavian countries. During this time, he continued his anti-fascist activities and supported the resistance against the Nazis.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
