      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Return to Germany</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, Brandt returned to Germany and entered the political arena as a member of the Social Democratic Party (SPD). He quickly became an important figure in Berlin's political landscape and was elected Mayor of West Berlin in 1957, a position he held during the critical period of the Berlin Wall's construction in 1961.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chancellor of West Germany</strong><br>\nWilly Brandt became the Chancellor of West Germany in 1969. He was the first Social Democrat in the post-war period to hold this office. His leadership was marked by a policy known as \"Ostpolitik,\" which aimed to improve relations with East Germany and the Soviet bloc.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ostpolitik and the Nobel Peace Prize</strong><br>\nOstpolitik was a groundbreaking move towards reconciliation with the Eastern Bloc during the Cold War. It resulted in several agreements that eased tensions and improved communication and travel between East and West Germany. In 1971, Brandt was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for his efforts in reducing Cold War tensions.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
