          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Domestic Policies</strong><br>\nWithin West Germany, Brandt's government implemented several progressive reforms, including improvements in social welfare programs, the expansion of education, and the liberalization of laws regarding citizenship and divorce. These reforms were part of his broader vision to modernize German society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Resignation and Later Years</strong><br>\nBrandt's chancellorship came to an abrupt end in 1974 when he resigned after it was revealed that one of his close aides was an East German spy. However, he continued to be active in politics, serving as the chairman of the SPD until 1987 and promoting international cooperation and peace.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nWilly Brandt's legacy is one of a statesman who worked tirelessly for peace and understanding between East and West during one of the most tension-filled eras of modern history. He is remembered for his courageous stance against Nazism, his visionary leadership in government, and his relentless pursuit of reconciliation during the Cold War.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
